  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe encounter a challenging optimization problem for a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap integral over circular shifts, a refined Erd\u0151s problem. The current record is about 0.380898, and our goal is to find a candidate with a lower value, ideally approaching 0.3808. The optimization landscape is highly non-convex with thousands of local minima, demanding a robust, multi-faceted approach.\n\nOur strategy targets the core issue: the overlap integral being the maximum lag in the correlation between h and (1-h). We need a method that explores structure beyond typical gradient descent and stochastic methods, leveraging the periodic boundary conditions and integral constraint. The key is combining multi-resolution analysis, dimension-adaptive sampling, and structure-constrained optimization, moving away from problem-specific heuristics toward generic solver interactions.\n\nInstead of recurrent perturbations, we fix a candidate resolution, guide structure via eigenvectors (Fourier-filtering), and locally optimize. We first encode structure by projecting the candidate onto low-frequency components, then refine with constrained optimization, and finally validate with a high-precision, dimension-consistent algorithm. This handles the curse of mobility by staying close to directional derivatives.\n\nMathematical specification\nRepresentation: h is an array of length n_points (initially doubling the provided 480 samples), with values in [0,1] and sum 1. We use a \"directions\" matrix D derived from eigenvectors (Fourier basis) to constrain candidates to a low-rank space.  \n\nObjective: minimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over k \u2208 \u211d, with domain [0,2]. Equivalently, we minimize the maximum circular correlation element between h and (1-h).  \n\nConstraints:  \n- Element-wise: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1  \n- Integral: \u2211h[i] = n_points/2  \n\nDerivation: The objective is non-convex due to products and arbitrary shifts. We approximate it with differentiable smooth_max_obj (max correlation penalized by log-sum-exp), guaranteeing we select the true maximum. The structure-constrained solution via eigenvectors (Fourier modes) enforces integral constraints automatically.  \n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Inherit pre-imported functions (smooth_max_obj_grad, project_to_sum)  \n2. Take initial_h_values (if available) or create a uniform h  \n3. Target resolution: double to 960, then check time before increasing again  \n4. Project initial_h for new resolution (if doubled) and adjust sum  \n5. Solve with constrained optimization:\n   - Construct initialization matrix via Fourier modes (n_modes = min(n_points, 40))\n   - Sample from projected space to avoid traps\n   - Use SLSQP or COBYLA to enforce constraints\n6. Sanitize use of linear constraint: maintain sum via project_to_sum  \n7. Handle time: vote for more samples in constrained search if budget tight  \n8. Optional: clip values between 0/1 automatically at iteration boundaries  \n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n- Tune n_modes from 30 to 50 as a function of n_points (fallback to 40 if >150)  \n- Gracefully degrade to random sampling if structure matrix fails (nudge to Fourier space)  \n- Monitor constraint % violations (aim < 1e-4)  \n- Target corner cases (min/max shifts) via round-robin decoding of lags  \n- Load new resolution when normalized computation time drops below 0.25 * max_time  \n\nEstimate doubling steps: first doubling (480\u2192960)+project \u2192 -35% error, second (960\u21921920) often \u22651 point improvement. Verify time norms: each correlation at n_peak is O(n_peak log\tn_peak). Program must complete before time limit expires.  \n\nFinal check: always enforce dimensionality if feasibility requires. Use fallback if all else fails, then output best candidate found.",
